<GuideLayout title="Gravel Sizes: Read the Product Gradation" metaDescription="Distinguish nominal stone sizes, sieve designations, fines and local trade names before choosing a material quantity tool." canonicalPath="/gravel-sizes" guideTitle="Gravel Sizes: Read the Product Gradation" guideSubtitle="Distinguish nominal stone sizes, sieve designations, fines and local trade names before choosing a material quantity tool." standardBadge="Read the actual product specification" readingTime="4 min read" lastUpdated="November 1, 2026" calculatorsSupported={calculatorsSupported}>

<section aria-labelledby="three-labels"><h2 id="three-labels">Separate three kinds of product labels</h2>
<p>A label such as “¾-inch gravel,” a grading such as #57, and a regional name such as crusher run answer different questions. The first suggests a size, the second points to a grading system, and the third may describe a locally sold blend. None alone establishes the material’s mineral type, washing, bulk weight or correct use on your site.</p>
<div class="table-responsive"><table><caption>Questions to ask about the words on an aggregate quote</caption><thead><tr><th scope="col">Label type</th><th scope="col">Example</th><th scope="col">Information still needed</th></tr></thead><tbody>
<tr><th scope="row">Nominal product size</th><td>¾-inch stone</td><td>Actual grading, oversize and smaller fractions, fines and wash status</td></tr>
<tr><th scope="row">Numbered size designation</th><td>#57 or #67</td><td>Governing specification, sieve results, source and intended layer</td></tr>
<tr><th scope="row">Minus product</th><td>¾-inch minus</td><td>The complete particle-size blend and whether its fines meet the project requirements</td></tr>
<tr><th scope="row">Regional or supplier name</th><td>QP, DGA, 21-A or CA-6</td><td>Exact product designation; names from different agencies are not automatically interchangeable</td></tr>
</tbody></table></div></section>
<section aria-labelledby="sieves"><h2 id="sieves">Read openings and percentages together</h2>
<p>Sieve analysis separates a sample through a series of openings and reports the proportion passing them. A larger opening passes more of a given sample. Numbered US sieves use their designated opening dimensions; the number is not an inch fraction or a stone’s diameter.</p>
<p>Ask for the current grading sheet and the specification used to evaluate it. Compare the complete required sieve series, not only the largest stone visible on the pile. <a href="https://www.pa.gov/agencies/penndot/programs-and-doing-business/construction/aggregate-plant-book">PennDOT’s producer records</a> list separate grading worksheets for #57, #67, other coarse aggregates and base products. Its <a href="https://www.ecamms.penndot.pa.gov/Public/Pages/Bulletins/BulletinSearch.aspx?BulletinTypeKey=1">Bulletin 14 search</a> is a source for Pennsylvania aggregate approvals.</p></section>
<section aria-labelledby="fines"><h2 id="fines">Clean grading and dense grading serve different layers</h2>
<p>An open-graded product has relatively few smaller particles between the larger pieces. A dense-graded product includes a broader blend and fines that can help particles pack together. The actual grading and fines content affect performance along with shape, moisture, confinement and the ground underneath.</p>
<p>Confirm washing and measured fines separately. A size number does not guarantee that stone is washed or establish its drainage rate. For a drain, follow the pipe and filter detail; for a base, follow the required product, thickness and placement procedure. The <a href="/angular-vs-rounded-rock">particle-shape comparison</a> explains another part of that choice.</p></section>
<section aria-labelledby="order"><h2 id="order">Turn the product choice into a quantity</h2>
<ol><li>Identify the actual product and layer specification.</li><li>Measure the finished footprint and selected layer thickness.</li><li>Calculate yards from geometry, then add a clearly defined loose-order allowance if needed.</li><li>Convert yards to US short tons with that supplier’s loose bulk weight, or use the bag’s stated cubic-foot yield.</li></ol>
<p>A 3-inch layer over 200 sq ft needs 1.85 yd³ before extra, whether the selected product is rounded or angular. Its estimated tons depend on loose bulk density. Particle size can change the thickness you need to select; it does not change the geometric conversion for a specified area and depth.</p>
<p>Use the <a href="/gravel-calculator">gravel calculator</a> for a loose layer, the <a href="/crusher-run-calculator">crusher run calculator</a> for a specified base, or the <a href="/57-stone-size-guide">#57 guide</a> for the grading and ordering checks behind that particular designation.</p></section>

